Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:04.50,DioramaTitle,,0,0,0,,{\b1\an2}JULIUS CAESAR DIDN'T CROSS THE RUBICON TO SEIZE POWER—HE DID IT TO STAY OUT OF A JAIL CELL.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:04.50,0:00:12.00,DioramaTitle,,0,0,0,,{\b1\an2}IN 49 BC, THE ROMAN SENATE CORNERED HIM. IF CAESAR LAID DOWN HIS COMMAND, HIS ENEMIES WERE WAITING WITH A STACK OF LAWSUITS THAT WOULD END HIS LIFE.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.00,0:00:18.50,DioramaTitle,,0,0,0,,{\b1\an2}HE BEGGED FOR A COMPROMISE; THEY DEMANDED TOTAL SURRENDER. FACED WITH POLITICAL EXECUTION OR HIGH TREASON, CAESAR CHOSE THE RIVER.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.50,0:00:25.00,DioramaTitle,,0,0,0,,{\b1\an2}BUT HERE’S THE TWIST: HE CLAIMED HIS 'DIGNITAS'—HIS PERSONAL EGO—MATTERED MORE THAN THE REPUBLIC ITSELF.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.00,0:00:32.00,DioramaTitle,,0,0,0,,{\b1\an2}WAS HE A VISIONARY HERO OR JUST A CORNERED CRIMINAL? YOU DECIDE. BECAUSE FOR CAESAR, THE DIE WAS CAST THE MOMENT HE REALIZED...